WEBVTT

00:29:28.888 --> 00:29:30.849
So they believed in that redemptive power

00:29:30.873 --> 00:29:32.613
and saw it as a tough-minded virtue.

00:29:32.637 --> 00:29:35.401
Dietrich Bonhoeffer was fighting
the Nazis in World War II.

00:29:35.425 --> 00:29:37.007
He had the exact same philosophy.

00:29:37.031 --> 00:29:40.176
Gandhi -- a lot of the people who
actually believe in social change

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:41.984
and were, in some ways, the toughest.

00:29:42.008 --> 00:29:43.693
The idea of the beloved community

00:29:43.717 --> 00:29:45.030
is a King idea.

00:29:45.054 --> 00:29:48.584
CA: What's the fundamental difference
between his approach,

00:29:48.608 --> 00:29:50.358
Martin Luther King, Jr.'s approach,

00:29:50.382 --> 00:29:53.320
and social justice warriors
of today, in your view?

00:29:53.344 --> 00:29:55.975
DB: He always extended the circle of care.

00:29:55.999 --> 00:29:59.029
I would say that would be
the thing he did.

00:29:59.053 --> 00:30:03.184
He extended the circle of care
and he believed we're all God's children.